# Menu path from launch to the oracle mission, for both drivers: oracle_input.exe into the
# original under Wine, and the native client (sfcop --input; oracle/driver/run_native.sh).
#
# This file is an input of the oracle (its name is the trace header's `input`), so it uses only
# the steps both drivers play: window, sleep, click, waitfile. Waits are by the clock and sized
# for the slower of the two: the main menu ("Select Game") is up 15 s after launch under Wine
# (oracle/baseline/C_after_key, 2026-11-03) and 18 to 42 s on the native build (the boot time is
# an open integrator item), so the first click waits 50 s. A change here changes the trace's
# input: say why in the commit.
#
# Coordinates are the game's display pixels: an 800x600 display with the 640x480 menu centred,
# the same space as the Wine driver's client area and the native window's logical size.
# Calibrated on the native build, 2026-11-05, with window captures of each screen (A2's pull
# request): Select Game -> Single Player -> Select Skirmish -> the mission, which PLAY GAME starts
# at once (no further screen; the script fixes ships, map and positions).

window 120 Starfleet Command
sleep 50000

# --- main menu, "Select Game": SINGLE PLAYER opens the Single Player screen
click 237 232
sleep 8000

# --- Single Player: SKIRMISH opens Select Skirmish, whose list fills as the missions load
click 400 190
sleep 8000

# --- Select Skirmish: the first row of the list; "!Oracle Skirmish" sorts first
click 135 347
sleep 3000

# --- PLAY GAME starts the mission
click 655 443

# The game writes OracleTrace.txt once the mission has started (next to the exe under Wine; the
# native client looks the name up in its logs folder).
waitfile 300 C:\Games\SFCOP\OracleTrace.txt

# --- the mission runs 20 turns, about 12 minutes, writes END and ends itself; the Mission
# Debriefing screen then waits for DONE, which returns to the main menu. These steps are for a
# plain native run (--input alone), which plays the file to its end. An oracle run on the native
# client (--oracle, --record-trace) exits at END, before them. Under Wine, oracle/run_oracle.sh
# stops the game as soon as the log has its END record (or at ORACLE_TIMEOUT, default 1800 s, which
# is longer than boot plus mission), so the driver never reaches them there either; they cost
# nothing on a recording, and the driver would play them (its `sleep` takes any whole number of
# milliseconds) if the game were left running.
sleep 1200000
click 655 496
sleep 10000
